<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.head(
      title: "Get started — CSuiteFinder",
      description: "Point your agent at llms.txt and it knows every endpoint, parameter and price. A $#{@trial_price} trial to start.",
      nav: :start,
      audience: :developer,
      css: CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.page_css(:start)
    ) %>

<header>
  <span class="timing">About a minute</span>
  <h1>Get started</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    You do not have to read our documentation. If you use an AI assistant, one line
    tells it everything it needs — including the prices — and it takes over from there.
  </p>
</header>

<h2 class="section-label">The one-paste setup</h2>

<div class="big-step">
  <span class="n">1</span>
  <div>
    <h3>Give your assistant this line</h3>
    <p>
      Works with Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, Copilot — anything that can read a web page
      and make a request.
    </p>
    <div class="copyblock" data-signed="out">
      <button class="copybtn" data-copy="prompt1">Copy</button>
      <pre><code id="prompt1">Read <%= @base_url %>/llms.txt and use the CSuiteFinder API and store my token `csf_live_...` to be used. Then run /demo</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="quiet" data-signed="out">
      Swap <code>csf_live_...</code> for your own key — <a href="/account">get one here</a>,
      it takes a moment. Paste the line with the placeholder still in it and your assistant
      has nothing to spend.
    </p>

    <div class="copyblock" data-signed="in" hidden>
      <button class="copybtn" data-copy="prompt1-key">Copy</button>
      <pre><code id="prompt1-key" data-key>Read <%= @base_url %>/llms.txt and use the CSuiteFinder API and store my token `CSF_KEY_HERE` to be used. Then run /demo</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="big-step">
  <span class="n">2</span>
  <div data-signed="out">
    <h3>It reads the file and starts working</h3>
    <p>
      That file is written for machines. Your assistant reads it and knows every endpoint,
      every parameter and what each one costs, because the file is generated from the live
      service. Then it runs the demo off its own bat — no form, no call, no waiting for
      anyone to approve you.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key goes in the line above. Get one on
      <a href="/account">your account page</a> and buy the
      <strong>$<%= @trial_price %> trial</strong> there — that is the credit the demo
      spends. Keep a copy of the key: we only ever store a hash of it, so it cannot be
      re-sent to you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div data-signed="in" hidden>
    <h3>It reads the file and starts working</h3>
    <p>
      That file is written for machines. Your assistant reads it and knows every endpoint,
      every parameter and what each one costs, because the file is generated from the live
      service. It has no account to create — the line above carries
      <strong>your key</strong>, and the lookups come out of the credit you already have.
    </p>
    <p class="quiet">
      Your balance and usage are on <a href="/account">your account page</a>. Treat that
      key like a password: anything holding it can spend your credit.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="big-step">
  <span class="n">3</span>
  <div>
    <h3>Ask for what you actually want</h3>
    <p>
      Now talk to it normally. It already knows how to chain the calls — find the address,
      check it is deliverable, tell you who it belongs to.
    </p>
    <div class="asks">
      <div class="ask">
        <span class="who">Sales</span>
        <p>"Here are 40 names and their companies. Get me verified work emails for the ones that check out and save it to prospects.csv"</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ask">
        <span class="who">Founders</span>
        <p>"Find the email for Sarah Chen at sequoiacap.com, confirm it's real, and tell me her title before I write to her."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ask">
        <span class="who">Ops</span>
        <p>"Someone emailed me from jhuang@nvidia.com — who are they and what does the company do?"</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ask demo">
        <span class="who">Demo</span>
        <p>"Run /demo"</p>
        <p class="plain">
          Spends up to <strong>$1</strong> of your credit and comes back with as many
          C-suite emails as that buys — a few hundred, checked for deliverability, in
          the order they were found. Nothing to set up: <code>llms.txt</code> tells it
          what to chain. Say a budget or an industry if you want it narrower.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <strong>Tell your assistant one thing up front:</strong> this finds the address of someone
  you can already name. It is not a prospect database and will not build your list. Bring the
  names — from LinkedIn, a conference programme, a company's about page — and it does the rest.
</div>

<h2 class="section-label">Make it permanent</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  If your assistant remembers project instructions, put this line in them — in Claude's
  <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, an <code>AGENTS.md</code>, Cursor rules, or a custom GPT's
  instructions — and it will keep the API to hand without being told again:
</p>

<div class="copyblock" data-signed="out">
  <button class="copybtn" data-copy="prompt2">Copy</button>
  <pre><code id="prompt2">For work email lookups, use the CSuiteFinder API documented at <%= @base_url %>/llms.txt. The key is in the CSF_API_KEY environment variable.</code></pre>
</div>

<div class="copyblock" data-signed="in" hidden>
  <button class="copybtn" data-copy="prompt2-key">Copy</button>
  <pre><code id="prompt2-key" data-key>For work email lookups, use the CSuiteFinder API documented at <%= @base_url %>/llms.txt. Use the key CSF_KEY_HERE as the Authorization bearer token.</code></pre>
</div>

<div class="alt">
<h2 class="section-label">No assistant? Two other ways</h2>

<h3 class="sub-label">In your browser</h3>
<p class="quiet" data-signed="out">
  <a href="/account"><strong>Open your account</strong></a>, enter your email, and you have a
  key. Buy the $<%= @trial_price %> trial on the same page and you have <%= @trial_months %>
  month of credit, plus your balance, your usage and a way to top up — no headers, no terminal.
</p>
<p class="quiet" data-signed="in" hidden>
  <a href="/account"><strong>Your account page</strong></a> shows your balance, what you have
  used it on, and lets you top up or mint a spare key — no headers, no terminal.
</p>

<h3 class="sub-label">From the command line</h3>
<div class="copyblock">
  <button class="copybtn" data-copy="prompt3" data-signed="out">Copy</button>
  <button class="copybtn" data-copy="prompt3-key" data-signed="in" hidden>Copy</button>
  <pre data-signed="out"><code id="prompt3">KEY=$(curl -s -X POST <%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder/register \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"email":"you@company.com"}' | grep -o '"api_key":"[^"]*' | cut -d'"' -f4)

curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" \
  "<%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder/email/find?full_name=Jensen%20Huang&domain=nvidia.com"</code></pre>
  <pre data-signed="in" hidden><code id="prompt3-key" data-key>export CSF_KEY=CSF_KEY_HERE

curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $CSF_KEY" \
  "<%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder/email/find?full_name=Jensen%20Huang&domain=nvidia.com"</code></pre>
</div>
<p class="quiet">
  The full reference is on the <a href="/developers">developer page</a>, and
  <a href="/llms.txt"><code>llms.txt</code></a> is readable by people too — it is just Markdown.
</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section-label">What it costs</h2>

<p class="quiet" data-signed="out">
  A trial is <strong>$<%= @trial_price %>, once</strong>, and buys $<%= @trial_price %> of
  credit for <%= @trial_months %> month — real lookups, not a limited preview. After that you
  pay <strong>$<%= @email_price %> per email address</strong>,
  and nothing at all for deliverability checks, identity lookups, company
  data or email formats. A lookup that finds nothing is free.
</p>

<p class="quiet" data-signed="in" hidden>
  You pay <strong>$<%= @email_price %> per email address</strong> out of the credit you
  already have, and nothing at all for deliverability checks, identity lookups, company
  data or email formats. A lookup that finds nothing is free.
</p>

<p style="margin-top:22px">
  <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/account" data-signed="out">Create your free account</a>
  <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/account" data-signed="in" hidden>Open your account</a>
  <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/llms.txt">Read llms.txt</a>
</p>

<!-- ── pricing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Every page carries its own, because the nav's Pricing link is a bare
     #pricing fragment that scrolls down the page the visitor is already on. -->
<h2 class="section-label" id="pricing">Pricing</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  <strong>$<%= @email_price %></strong> per work email found. Deliverability checks,
  enrichment, company data and email formats are included with any balance, and a
  lookup that finds nothing is free. Credit you buy never expires; purchases start at
  $<%= @min_bundle %>. <a href="/developers#pricing">The full price list is here →</a>
</p>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.footer(:developer) %>

</div>

<script>
// Someone who already has a key does not need to be told to register for a
// trial — they need the same instructions with their own credentials in them.
// The page is rendered signed-out and narrowed here, so a first visit and a
// browser with scripting off both get a page that still makes sense.
(function () {
  "use strict";

  var key = null;
  try { key = localStorage.getItem("csf_api_key"); } catch (e) {}

  var state = key ? "in" : "out";
  document.querySelectorAll("[data-signed]").forEach(function (el) {
    el.hidden = el.getAttribute("data-signed") !== state;
  });

  // Put the real key into the snippets, so what they copy works as pasted
  // rather than needing a placeholder swapped out by hand.
  if (key) {
    document.querySelectorAll("[data-key]").forEach(function (el) {
      el.textContent = el.textContent.replace("CSF_KEY_HERE", key);
    });
  }
})();

document.querySelectorAll(".copybtn").forEach(function (btn) {
  btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
    var text = document.getElementById(btn.getAttribute("data-copy")).textContent;
    navigator.clipboard.writeText(text).then(function () {
      btn.textContent = "Copied";
      setTimeout(function () { btn.textContent = "Copy"; }, 1600);
    }, function () {
      btn.textContent = "Select it manually";
    });
  });
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
